   475	
   476	\begin{theorem}\label{t-c6}
   477	{\sc List $3$-Colouring} can be solved in polynomial time for $C_6$-free graphs of diameter at most $2$.
   478	\end{theorem}
   479	
   480	\begin{proof}
   481	Let $G=(V,E)$ be a $C_6$-free graph of diameter~$2$ with a list $3$-assignment~$L$. If $G$ is $C_5$-free, then we apply Theorem~\ref{t-c5}. 
   482	 If $G$ contains a $K_4$, then $G$ is not $3$-colourable and hence, $(G,L)$ is a no-instance of {\sc List $3$-Colouring}.
   483	 We check these properties in polynomial time. So, from now on, we assume that $G$ is a $K_4$-free graph that contains an induced $5$-vertex cycle~$C$, say with vertex set $N_0=\{x_1,\ldots,x_5\}$ in this order. Let $N_1$ be the set of vertices that do not belong to $C$ but that are adjacent to at least one vertex of $C$. Let $N_2=V\setminus (N_0\cup N_1)$  be the set of remaining vertices.
   484	
   485	As $N_0$ has size~$5$, we can apply a full $N_0$-propagation in polynomial time by Lemma~\ref{l-easy2}. By the same lemma we are done if we can prove that $(G,L)$ is $N_0$-terminal. We prove this claim below.
   486	
   487	For contradiction, assume that $(G,L)$ is not $N_0$-terminal. Then there must exist an $L$-promising $N_0$-precolouring $c$
   488	for which we obtain the $c$-propagated list assignment $L'_c$. By definition of $L'_c$ we find that $G$ contains a 
   489	vertex $v$ with $L'_c(v)=\{1,2,3\}$. Then $v\notin N_0$, as every $u\in N_0$ has $L'_c(u)=\{c(u)\}$. 
   490	Moreover, $v\notin N_1$, as vertices in $N_1$ have a list of size at most~$2$ after applying Rule~\ref{r-one2}.
   491	Hence, we find that $v\in N_2$. 
   492	
   493	We first note that some colour of $\{1,2,3\}$ appears exactly once on $N_0$, as $|N_0|=5$. Hence, we may assume without loss of generality that $c(x_1)=1$ and that $c(x_i)\in \{2,3\}$ for every $i\in \{2,3,4,5\}$.
   494	
   495	As $G$ has diameter $2$, there exists a vertex $y\in N_1$ that is adjacent to $x_1$ and~$v$. As $L_c'(v)=\{1,2,3\}$ and $c(x_1)=1$, we find that $L_c'(y)=\{2,3\}$. As $c(x_i)\in \{2,3\}$ for every $i\in \{2,3,4,5\}$, the
   496	 latter means that $y$ is not adjacent to any $x_i$ with $i\in \{2,3,4,5\}$. Hence, as $G$ has diameter~$2$, there exists a vertex $z\in N_1$ with $z\neq y$, such that $z$ is adjacent to $x_3$ and $v$. We assume without loss of generality that $c(x_3)=3$ and thus $c(x_2)=c(x_4)=2$ and thus $c(x_5)=3$.  As $L_c'(v)=\{1,2,3\}$ and $c(x_3)=3$, we find that $L_c'(z)=\{1,2\}$. 
   497	Hence, $z$ is not adjacent to any vertex of $\{x_1,x_2,x_4\}$.
   498	Now the set $\{x_1,x_2,x_3,z,v,y\}$ forms a cycle on six vertices. As $G$ is $C_6$-free, this cycle cannot be induced. Hence, the above implies that $y$ and $z$ must be adjacent; see also Figure~\ref{f-both}.
   499	
   500	As $G$ has diameter~$2$, there exists a vertex $w\in N_1$ that is adjacent to $x_4$ and $v$. As both $y$ and $z$ are not adjacent to $x_4$, we find that $w\notin \{y,z\}$.
   501	As $L'_c(v)=\{1,2,3\}$ and $c(x_4)=2$, we find that $L_c'(w)=\{1,3\}$.
   502	As $c(x_1)=1$ and $c(x_3)=c(x_5)=3$, the latter
   503	 implies that $w$ is not adjacent to any vertex of $\{x_1,x_3,x_5\}$. Consequently, $w$ must be adjacent to $y$, as otherwise the $6$-vertex cycle with vertex set $\{x_1,x_5,x_4,w,v,y\}$ would be induced, contradicting the $C_6$-freeness of $G$. We refer again to Figure~\ref{f-both} for a display of the situation.
   504	
   505	If $w$ and $z$ are adjacent, then $\{v,w,y,z\}$ induces a $K_4$, contradicting the $K_4$-freeness of $G$. 
   506	Hence, $w$ and $z$ are not adjacent.
   507	Then $\{v,w,y,z\}$ induces a diamond, in which $w$ and $z$ are the two non-adjacent vertices. 
   508	However, as $L_c'(w)=\{1,3\}$ and $L_c'(z)=\{1,2\}$, our algorithm would have applied Rule~\ref{r-diamond}. This would have resulted in lists of $w$ and $z$ that are both equal to $\{1,3\}\cap \{1,2\}=\{1\}$. Hence, we obtained a contradiction and conclude that $(G,L)$ is $N_0$-terminal. \qed
   509	\end{proof}
   510	
   511	\noindent
   512	Theorem~\ref{t-c4c7} is proven in a similar way as Theorem~\ref{t-c6}.
   513	
   514	\begin{theorem}\label{t-c4c7}
   515	{\sc List $3$-Colouring} can be solved in polynomial time for $(C_4,C_7)$-free graphs of diameter~$2$.
   516	\end{theorem}
   517	\begin{proof}
   518	Let $G=(V,E)$ be a $C_4$-free graph of diameter~$2$ with a list $3$-assignment~$L$. If $G$ is $C_5$-free, then we apply Theorem~\ref{t-c5}. Hence we may assume that $G$ contains an induced $5$-vertex cycle~$C$, say with vertex set $N_0=\{x_1,\ldots,x_5\}$ in this order. As before, we let $N_1$ be the set of vertices that do not belong to $C$ but that are adjacent to at least one vertex of $C$. We also let $N_2=V\setminus (N_0\cup N_1)$  denote the set of remaining vertices again.
   519	
   520	As $N_0$ has size~$5$, we can apply a full $N_0$-propagation in polynomial time by Lemma~\ref{l-easy2}. By the same lemma we are done if we can prove that $(G,L)$ is $N_0$-terminal. We prove this claim in exactly the same way in which we proved a similar claim in 
   521	the proof of Theorem~\ref{t-c6} except for the following differences:
   522	\begin{enumerate}
   523	\item instead of using the $6$-vertex set $\{x_1,x_2,x_3,z,v,y\}$ we use the $7$-vertex set 
   524	$\{x_1,x_5,x_4,x_3,z,v,y\}$ after observing that $z$ cannot be adjacent to $x_5$ due to the $C_4$-freeness of $G$, and
   525	\item instead of using the $6$-vertex set $\{x_1,x_5,x_4,w,v,y\}$ we use the $7$-vertex set $\{x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4,w,v,y\}$ after observing that $w$ cannot be adjacent to $x_2$, again due to the $C_4$-freeness of $G$.
   526	\end{enumerate}
   527	We refer again to Figure~\ref{f-both} for a display of the situation. \qed
   528	\end{proof}
   529	
   530	\section{The Extended Propagation Algorithm and Two Results}\label{s-pro2}
   531	
   532	For our next two results, we need a more sophisticated method. Let $(G,L)$ be an instance of {\sc List $3$-Colouring}. 
   533	Let $p$ be some positive constant. We consider each set $N_0\subseteq V(G)$ of size at most~$p$ and perform a full $N_0$-propagation. 
   534	Afterwards we say that we performed a {\it full $p$-propagation}.
   535	We say that $(G,L)$ is {\it $p$-terminal} if after the full $p$-propagation one of the following cases hold:
   536	
   537	\begin{enumerate}
   538	\item for some $N_0\subseteq V(G)$ with $|N_0|\leq c$, there is an $L$-promising $N_0$-precolouring~$c$, such that the propagation algorithm returns {\tt yes}; or
   539	\item for every set $N_0\subseteq V(G)$ with $|N_0|\leq c$ and every $L$-promising $N_0$-precolouring~$c$, the propagation algorithm returns {\tt no}.
   540	\end{enumerate}
   541	
   542	\noindent
   543	We can now prove the following lemma.
   544	
   545	\begin{lemma}\label{l-easy3}
   546	Let $(G,L)$ be an instance of {\sc List $3$-Colouring} and $p\geq 1$ be some constant.
   547	Performing a full $p$-propagation takes polynomial time. Moreover, if $(G,L)$ is $p$-terminal, then we have solved {\sc List $3$-Colouring} on instance $(G,L)$.
   548	\end{lemma}
   549	
   550	\begin{proof}
   551	For every set $N_0\subseteq V(G)$, a full $N_0$-propagation takes polynomial time by Lemma~\ref{l-easy2}.
   552	Then the first statement of the lemma follows from this observation and the fact that we need to perform $O(n^p)$ full $N_0$-propagations, which is a polynomial number, as $p$ is a constant.
   553	
   554	Now suppose that $(G,L)$ is $p$-terminal. First assume that for some $N_0\subseteq V(G)$ with $|N_0|\leq c$, there exists an $L$-promising $N_0$-precolouring $c$, such that the propagation algorithm returns {\tt yes}. Then $(G,L)$ is a yes-instance due to Lemma~\ref{l-easy}.
   555	Now assume that for every set $N_0\subseteq V(G)$ with $|N_0|\leq c$ and every  $L$-promising $N_0$-precolouring $c$, the propagation algorithm returns {\tt no}. Then $(G,L)$ is a no-instance. This follows from Lemma~\ref{l-easy} combined with the observation that if $(G,L)$ was a yes-instance, the restriction of a colouring $c$ that respects $L$ to any set $N_0$ of size at most $p$ would be an $L$-promising $N_0$-precolouring of $G$. \qed
   556	\end{proof}
   557	
   558	\noindent
   559	In our next two algorithms, we perform a full $p$-propagation for some appropriate constant~$p$. If we find that an instance $(G,L)$ is $p$-terminal, then we are done by Lemma~\ref{l-easy3}. In the other case, we exploit the new information on the structure of~$G$ that we obtain from the fact that $(G,L)$ is not $p$-terminal.
   560	
   561	\begin{theorem}\label{t-c4c8}
   562	{\sc List $3$-Colouring} can be solved in polynomial time for $(C_4,C_8)$-free graphs of diameter~$2$.
   563	\end{theorem}
   564	
   565	\begin{proof}
   566	Let $G=(V,E)$ be a $(C_4,C_8)$-free graph of diameter~$2$ with a list $3$-assignment $L$. If $G$ is $C_6$-free, then we apply Theorem~\ref{t-c6}. 
   567	 If $G$ contains a $K_4$, then $G$ is not $3$-colourable and hence, $(G,L)$ is a no-instance of {\sc List $3$-Colouring}. We check these properties in polynomial time. So, from now on, we assume that $G$ is a $K_4$-free graph that contains at least one induced cycle on six vertices.
   568	 
   569	We set $p=6$ and perform a full $p$-propagation. This takes polynomial time by Lemma~\ref{l-easy}. By the same lemma, we have solved {\sc List $3$-Colouring} on $(G,L)$ if $(G,L)$ is $p$-terminal. Suppose we find that $(G,L)$ is not $p$-terminal.
   570	
   571	We first prove the following claim.
   572	
   573	\medskip
   574	\noindent
   575	{\it Claim 1. For each induced $6$-vertex cycle $C$, the propagation algorithm returned {\tt no} for every $V(C)$-promising colouring $c$ that assigns the same colour $i$ on two vertices of $C$ that have a common neighbour on $C$.}
   576	
   577	\medskip
   578	\noindent
   579	We prove Claim~1 as follows.
   580	Consider an induced $6$-vertex cycle~$C$, say with vertex set $N_0=\{x_1,\ldots,x_6\}$ in this order. Let $N_1$ be the set of vertices that do not belong to $C$ but that are adjacent to at least one vertex of $C$. Let $N_2=V\setminus (N_0\cup N_1)$  be the set of remaining vertices.
   581	For contradiction, let $c$ be a $V(C)$-promising colouring that assigns two vertices of $C$ with a common neighbour on $C$ the same colour, say $c(x_1)=1$ and $c(x_3)=1$, such that a full $c$-propagation does not yield a {\tt no} output. As $(G,L)$ is not $p$-terminal, this means
   582	that we obtained the $c$-propagated list assignment $L'_c$. By definition of $L'_c$ we find that $G$ contains  
   583	a vertex $v$ with $L'_c(v)=\{1,2,3\}$. Then $v\notin N_0$, as every $u\in N_0$ has $L'_c(u)=\{c(u)\}$. 
   584	Moreover, $v\notin N_1$, as vertices in $N_1$ have a list of size at most~$2$ after applying Rule~\ref{r-one2}.
   585	Hence, we find that $v\in N_2$. 
   586	
   587	As $G$ has diameter~$2$, there exist a vertex $y\in N_1$ that is adjacent to both~$v$ and~$x_1$.
   588	As $c(x_1)=1$, we find that $c(x_2)\in \{2,3\}$ and $c(x_6)\in \{2,3\}$.
   589	As $c(x_3)=1$, we find that $c(x_4)\in \{2,3\}$. Hence, $y$ is not adjacent to any vertex of $\{x_2,x_4,x_6\}$; 
   590	otherwise $y$ would have a list of size~$1$ due to Rule~\ref{r-one2}, and by the same rule, $v$ would have a list of size~$2$.
   591	We note that $y$ is not adjacent to $x_3$  
   592	or $x_5$ either, as otherwise $\{x_1,x_2,x_3,y\}$ or $\{x_1,x_6,x_5,y\}$ induces a $C_4$, contradicting the $C_4$-freeness of $G$.
   593	
   594	As $G$ has diameter~$2$ and $yx_3\notin E$, there exists a vertex $y'\in N_1\setminus \{y\}$ that is adjacent to both $v$ and $x_3$. By the same arguments as above, $y'$ is not adjacent to any vertex of $\{x_1,x_2,x_4,x_5,x_6\}$.
   595	If $y$ and $y'$ are adjacent, then $v$ would have list $\{1\}$ due to Rule~\ref{r-bull}.
   596	Hence $y$ and $y'$ are not adjacent. However, we now find that $\{x_1,y,v,y',x_3,x_4,x_5,x_6\}$ induces a $C_8$, contradicting the $C_8$-freeness of $G$; see also Figure~\ref{f-c8}. This proves Claim~1.
   597	
