   962	\medskip
   963	\noindent
   964	{\bf Theorem~\ref{t-hard} (restated).}
   965	{\it For every even integer $t\geq 6$, {\sc $3$-Colouring} is \NP-complete on the class of $(C_4,C_6,\ldots,C_t)$-free graphs of diameter~$4$.}
   966	
   967	\begin{proof}
   968	Note that the problem is readily seen to be in \NP.
   969	To prove \NP-hardness we modify the standard reduction for {\sc Colouring} from the \NP-complete problem {\sc Not-All-Equal $3$-Satisfiability}~\cite{Sc78}, where each variable appears in at most three clauses. 
   970	So, given a CNF formula $\phi$, we first construct a graph~$G$ as follows (see also Figure~\ref{f-hardhard}):
   971	
   972	\begin{itemize}
   973	    \item add literal vertices $v_{i}$ and  $v'_{i}$ for each variable $x_i$;
   974	    \item add an edge between each $v_{i}$ and  $v'_{i}$;
   975	    \item add a vertex $z$ adjacent to every $v_i$ and every $v_i'$;
   976	    \item for each clause $C_i$ add a triangle $T_i$ with clause vertices $c_{i_1}, c_{i_2}, c_{i_3}$;
   977	    \item fix an arbitrary order of the literals $x_{i_1}, x_{i_2}, x_{i_3}$ of $C_i$ and for $j\in \{1,2,3\}$, add the edge $v_{i_j}c_{i_j}$ if $x_{i_j}$ is positive and the edge $v_{i_j}'c_{i_j}$ if $x_{i_j}$ is negative.
   978	\end{itemize}
   979	
   980	\noindent
   981	It is well known that $\phi$ has a truth assignment $\tau$ such that each clause contains at least one true literal and at least one false literal (call such a $\tau$ satisfying) if and only if $G$ has a $3$-colouring. For completeness we give a proof below.
   982	
   983	First suppose $\phi$ has a satisfying truth assignment. Colour vertex $z$ with colour~$1$, each true literal with colour $2$ and each false literal with colour $3$. Then, as each clause has a true literal and a false literal, each triangle $T_i$ has neighbours in two different colours. Hence, we can complete the $3$-colouring.
   984	
   985	Now suppose $G$ has a $3$-colouring. Say $z$ is assigned colour $1$. Then each literal vertex has either colour $2$ or colour $3$. Moreover, each $T_i$ must be adjacent to at least one literal vertex coloured~$2$ and to at least one literal vertex  coloured~$3$.  Hence, the truth assignment that sets literals whose vertices are coloured with colour $2$ to be true and those coloured with colour $3$ to be false is satisfying. 
   986	
   987	As every clause vertex is adjacent to a literal vertex and literal vertices are adjacent to $z$, every vertex has distance at most $2$ from $z$. So $G$ has diameter~$4$.
   988	
   989	We modify $G$ into a graph $G'$: for some $p\geq 0$, subdivide each edge $v_{i_j}c_{i_j}$ and each edge $v_{i_j}'c_{i_j}$ 
   990	$p$ times and make each newly introduced vertex adjacent to $z$; see also Figure~\ref{f-hardhard}. Then $G'$ has a $3$-colouring if and only if $G$ has a $3$-colouring, as the new vertices will be alternatingly coloured by $2$ and $3$ if $z$ has colour~$1$.
   991	Moreover, $G'$ still has diameter~$4$, and it can be readily checked that every induced cycle of~$G$ of length at most $p$ is either a $C_3$ (either a triangle $T_i$ or a triangle containing $z$) or a $C_5$ (which must contain~$z$). As we can make $p$ arbitrarily large, the result follows.\qed
   992	\end{proof}
