NOTES = """\
Section 1 fixes the native graph conventions: colorings use exactly the colors
{1,2,3}, graphs are simple, and a plane graph includes an actual embedding.
Definitions 2.4--2.6 introduce the object used here.  Its vertices are frontier
edges in cyclic order on a circle; ordinary edges are straight chords, loops lie
outside; a perfect quasi-matching covers every vertex once and is non-crossing.
This family uses the loop-free subfamily, without reducing the object to SAT.

The prior edge-coloring triage cannot support Track A.  Theorem 1 is proved by
six constant-size reducible patterns, recursive deletion, and finite Kempe
switches; the discharging argument makes one of those local reductions
unavoidable.  Thus the proof is a constructive polynomial procedure on the
paper's planar subcubic girth-seven regime.  The NP-hardness statements in
Section 3 concern the broader classes of general and triangle-free subcubic
graphs, not this distribution.

Section 3 also states that the authors reduced non-crossing perfect
quasi-matching to CNF-SAT and only needed auxiliary pseudographs of at most six
vertices.  That tiny regime is easy, so the ladder grows the same native object
and declares Track B: interval dynamic programming is an exact O(n^3)
reference algorithm.  The generator first samples several reflection
permutations between even and odd cyclic positions.  Every reflection supplies
an entire non-crossing perfect matching.  It then adds disjoint cyclic-shift
permutations, each also a perfect matching with the same local degree
contribution but whose chords cross.  One sampled reflection is retained as
the planted certificate; no search is run.

The outlier attack is defeated because every vertex has the same allowed
degree.  Shortest-chord, longest-chord, lowest-label, adjacent-pair, constant
cyclic-distance, and randomized greedy attacks mix reflection and shift orbits.
The compact successful route compares consecutive even-position
neighbourhoods: a reflection neighbour moves backward while a shift neighbour
moves forward.  One recovered reflection orbit is a witness.  The canonical
key removes arbitrary vertex names, edge order, cyclic rotation, and reversal
by normalizing the complete embedded chord set.
